Let $D^2$ denote the closed unit disk in $\mathbb{R}^2$ equipped with the Lebesgue (area) measure $\mu$, and let $\mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2)$ denote the space of $C^\infty$ diffeomorphisms of $D^2$ that preserve $\mu$, endowed with the $C^\infty$ topology. For $f \in \mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2)$, let $h_\mu(f)$ be the metric (Kolmogorov–Sinai) entropy of $f$ with respect to $\mu$, and let $\lambda_+(f,\mu) = \int \max(\lambda^+(f,x),0)\,d\mu(x)$ be the integrated positive Lyapunov exponent, so that by the Ruelle inequality and Pesin formula one has $h_\mu(f) \le \lambda_+(f,\mu)$, and in the area-preserving setting $h_\mu(f)=0$ if and only if $\lambda_+(f,\mu)=0$. Define
$$G = \{ f \in \mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2) : h_\mu(f) = 0 \}.$$
Decide which of the following two mutually exclusive Baire alternatives holds:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $G$ is a dense $G_\delta$ subset of $\mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2)$;
\item[(b)] there exist a nonempty $C^\infty$-open set $U \subset \mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2)$ and a constant $\delta > 0$ such that $h_\mu(g) \ge \delta$ for every $g \in U$.

